{
  "analysis": "The review describes paid Poké Balls being consumed during chance-based capture attempts, with failed captures causing more purchases; this satisfies Gambling. Pay to Progress is rejected because the payment is internal to the chance-based retry mechanic. Intermediate Currency and Waste Aversion are rejected because no real-money conversion into currency or stranded currency is described. The denial that the reviewer wrote the review, combined with the fabricated claim about talking to players, describes a review posted under the player's identity, satisfying Friend Spam / Impersonation.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "since I started purchasing, items \"poke\" balls that when I tried to make a capture that they keep breaking out so I go through the balls faster so I have to purchase more",
      "rationale": "Paid Poké Balls are used for repeated chance-based capture attempts, with failure prompting further purchases."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_FriendSpamImpersonation",
      "span": "I DID NOT WRITE THIS REVIEW.....I DIDN'T TALK TO SEVERAL PLAYERS BECAUSE I DO NOT KNOW ANYONE",
      "rationale": "The review states that content was posted under the player's identity and falsely attributed social interaction to them."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes purchased or acquired robots becoming weaker after game changes, which satisfies Power Creep. It also describes an algorithmic matchmaking behavior that places the player against substantially stronger opponents, satisfying Illusion of Control because outcome difficulty is attributed to hidden matchmaking manipulation. Competing against real players is explicitly described, so Competition applies. Spending thousands to max out options is presented as the route to winning, satisfying Pay to Progress. No labels for grinding, gambling, recurring fees, or FOMO apply because those mechanics are not described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PowerCreep",
      "span": "after each change, my robots get weaker and weaker",
      "rationale": "Previously acquired robots lose value after game changes."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the algorithm will put you against real players that are way over powered for you to fight",
      "rationale": "The review attributes difficulty and outcomes to manipulated or hidden matchmaking."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "put you against real players",
      "rationale": "The game explicitly matches the player into competition with other players."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "unless you're willing to dump thousands into it and max out every option",
      "rationale": "Spending money to max out options is described as necessary to win or progress competitively."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes players using purchases to advance through the game, which satisfies Pay to Progress. The references to hacking describe player misconduct rather than a game mechanic, so no social or technical label applies. “Grinding” is a general complaint without a repeated task linked to progress, so T_Grinding is rejected under its boundary rule. The inventory, bots, weapons, and useless gear do not establish reward randomness, power creep, collection pressure, or currency conversion.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "buy their way through the game",
      "rationale": "This describes spending money as a route to advance through the game."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes payment as necessary to obtain newly introduced robots and frames the game as pay-to-win, satisfying M_PayToProgress. It also states that a purchased robot is nerfed one month later while a newer robot is introduced, showing devaluation of an acquired item and satisfying M_PowerCreep. Competition is mentioned through clans and other players defeating the reviewer, but no specific competitive game mechanic is clearly described beyond ordinary opposition, so S_Competition is rejected under R2. Lag is attributed to a technical fault and is not coded under R11. No currency conversion, gambling, advertising, scheduling, or other qualifying mechanic is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "They will introduce a new robot and you will spend money to get it.",
      "rationale": "The span describes spending money to obtain an in-game robot that provides gameplay advantage or progress."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PowerCreep",
      "span": "And then one month later, the robot you have get nerfed and another new robot introduced.",
      "rationale": "The span describes an acquired robot being weakened after a newer robot is introduced, reducing its value over time."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes repetitive effort invested toward progression and an update that devalues previously achieved progress by giving newer players equivalent hero-equipment progress for free. These support T_Grinding and M_PowerCreep. I rejected M_RecurringFee because passes are mentioned but no mechanic encouraging play to justify recurring spending is described; M_PayToProgress because spending is reported without being stated as required for progression; T_InfiniteTreadmill because being years from maxing out does not establish an unattainable end state; and T_DailyRewards because daily play is not linked to a reward or penalty for missing days.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "After a year of grinding",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes extended repetitive effort invested in achieving game progress."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PowerCreep",
      "span": "Supercell just released an update that invalidates most of the progress I achieved. New players get my current level of hero equipment progress for free, and can easily overtake me",
      "rationale": "The update makes previously achieved progress less valuable by allowing newer players to catch up or surpass it for free."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes real money being exchanged for in-game coins, which satisfies Intermediate Currency. It does not clearly describe gambling, a progress gate, randomised reward delivery, or currency stranded through a pricing mismatch; the coins being consumed in a round is not Waste Aversion under its boundary rule.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "$20 for only 100k or so coins",
      "rationale": "This describes acquiring in-game coins through a real-money purchase."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes progress being blocked at specific levels unless the player spends coins or buys more coins. This satisfies Pay to Progress because coins are required to unlock further progress and purchasing them is the paid bypass. Buying coins also establishes Intermediate Currency because real-world purchasing is used to acquire in-game currency. Daily Rewards and Advertisement are only requested as possible solutions, so they are excluded under R12. Grinding, Infinite Treadmill, FOMO, and Waste Aversion are not supported by a described mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "I can't seem to get past it without using coins, which I have run out of, or buying coins",
      "rationale": "The player describes purchasing or spending coins as necessary to pass blocked levels and continue progressing."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "buying coins",
      "rationale": "The span describes acquiring in-game coins through a purchase."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes requesting a stamp from the clan, which is a resource-help exchange and satisfies Reciprocity. It also describes promotional wording that misleads the player about the amount of extra coins received, satisfying Aesthetic Manipulation because the deception is in the text. I considered UI Misdirection for the controls that change to a higher setting, but the review does not clearly connect this to a purchase or to an obstructed purchase opt-out, so it is rejected under R2 and the UI Misdirection boundary rule. Intermediate Currency, Pay to Progress, Gambling, and Easy to Purchase are not supported because no real-money conversion, progress gate, wager, or purchase-interception mechanic is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Reciprocity",
      "span": "ask our clan for a stamp",
      "rationale": "The player requests an in-game resource or favor from existing clan members."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_AestheticManipulation",
      "span": "The wording makes you think you are going to get more than you think",
      "rationale": "Misleading promotional wording exploits the player's interpretation to influence expectations about the reward."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes repeated purchase prompts for rolls that appear whenever the player runs out of rolls, which is an insufficient-resource interception and fits M_EasyToPurchase. T_Advertisement is rejected because the prompts are purchase offers, not ads or rewarded ad viewing. M_PayToProgress is rejected because the review does not say payment is required to continue or progress. No timer or regeneration schedule is described, so T_PlayingByAppointment is rejected.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_EasyToPurchase",
      "span": "we don't need 6+ thrown at us every time we are out of rolls",
      "rationale": "Purchase offers are repeatedly pushed at the player when their rolls are depleted, intercepting play at an insufficient-resource point."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes competitive matchmaking against other players and alleges that matchmaking intentionally assigns bad teammates based on the player's performance, which fits Illusion of Control because the outcome-determination system is portrayed as manipulated under a hidden skill-based rule. General anger, claims that the game wants money, copying another game, and wasting time or energy do not describe codable mechanics under R2; no specific payment route, repetitive task, timer, or resource mechanic is identified.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "The match making, completely illogical",
      "rationale": "Matchmaking describes the game placing players into matches involving competition against other players."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "if you play good,game will give you bad teammates expecting you to carry and if you play bad,game will give you more bad teammates saying that it's so called \"skill based matching\"",
      "rationale": "The span alleges that matchmaking outcomes are deliberately tuned according to the player's performance through a concealed or misleading skill-based system."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review names a gacha mechanic, which triggers P_RewardMania under the gacha-mechanics indicator. Power creep is explicitly described as absent ('doesnt use power creep'), so M_PowerCreep is rejected under R4. M_Gambling is rejected because the review does not clearly describe real-money spending or a wager; 'buy into the gatcha' is insufficiently explicit under the Gambling boundary rule.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "buy into the gatcha",
      "rationale": "This span names participation in a gacha mechanic, an indicator of Reward Mania."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes randomized sticker rewards producing duplicates and a collection goal that cannot be completed without payment. These satisfy Reward Mania, Complete the Collection, and Pay to Progress. Gambling is rejected because no wager or chance-based purchase is described; Deceptive Luxury is rejected because no scarcity tied to price or purchase is stated.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "always get duplicates",
      "rationale": "Describes repeated duplicate drops from a randomized reward or collection system."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "finish all the sets",
      "rationale": "Explicitly describes the compulsion or goal of acquiring items to complete all collections."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "never lets you finish all the sets unless you pay",
      "rationale": "States that further collection progress is contingent on making a payment."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an initial game download followed by an additional in-game download, which matches Fragmented Downloads because the true download requirements are revealed only after starting. The matchmaking problem is attributed to a Wi-Fi/technical issue, so it is not coded under any gameplay mechanic label per R11. No advertisement, wait timer, or progress-gating mechanic is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "Tech_FragmentedDownloads",
      "span": "I thought I already download the file but I still get an additional download",
      "rationale": "This describes an additional in-game download after the initial file, indicating fragmented disclosure of the game's download requirements."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
