  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes repeated near-misses and explicitly attributes them to rigging, which supports Illusion of Control and Optimism and Frequency Biases. The constant purchase prompts are solicitation volume without a described interception or friction-reducing purchase mechanism, so EasyToPurchase is rejected under its boundary rule. PayToProgress is rejected because the review does not state that payment is required to advance, only that the game seems designed to make the player spend.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "it feels like almost every game is rigged to make you spend",
      "rationale": "The reviewer asserts that the game manipulates or rigs outcomes to create a misleading impression and induce further play or spending."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_OptimismAndFrequencyBiases",
      "span": "a level is failed 1 or 2 moves from winning. 20 or 30 times in a row",
      "rationale": "This describes repeated engineered or clustered near-wins that can make success feel unusually frequent or imminent."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes progression as requiring wealth or spending: being rich is presented as the only way to level up, and spending is explicitly linked to upgrading. This satisfies M_PayToProgress. I rejected M_IntermediateCurrency because no real-money purchase of an in-game currency is described; T_Grinding because no repetitive activity is specified; S_Competition because multiplayer competition is not described; and M_PowerCreep because no purchased item loses value over time.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "Hope your rich, cause that the only way your gonna level up in the beginning.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer states that being wealthy is the only way to level up, making payment or spending a route required for progression."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a 10-hour wait to replenish a full set of spins, which is an in-game timer imposing when the player can resume play, so T_PlayingByAppointment applies. The statements that the game wants spending and that bought spins yielded nothing do not establish payment as required for progress, a real-money-to-currency conversion, gambling, or realised currency waste. The complaint about extra money taken is a transaction dispute without a qualifying purchase-interface mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "takes 10hrs just to get a full set of spins",
      "rationale": "Describes an in-game timer requiring the player to wait 10 hours for spins to replenish before continuing play."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an invitation mechanic tied to getting friends to join Facebook, which satisfies Forced Fellowship despite the reported reward failure. I rejected Friend Spam / Impersonation because no message, post, or invitation sent through the player's account is described. I rejected Gambling and Reward Mania because 'played max bet' and 'Stickers are duplicates all the way' do not clearly establish a qualifying wager or randomized reward-delivery mechanic. The internet connection error is attributed to a technical fault, so it is not coded under a design label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_ForcedFellowship",
      "span": "no rewards for inviting friends and getting them to join facebook",
      "rationale": "Describes inviting friends to join the game or its social network in connection with an expected in-game reward."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a 30-day expiration rule that removes collected cards and forces the collection process to restart, which satisfies Playing by Appointment because progress/rewards are governed by a game-set deadline and loss. It also explicitly describes the goal of collecting all cards, satisfying Complete the Collection. Deceptive Luxury is rejected because the rarity is not tied to price or purchase. Reward Mania and Gambling are rejected because no randomised delivery mechanism or monetary stake is described. Reciprocity and Friend Spam / Impersonation are rejected because mentioning a Facebook trading group does not describe compelled exchange or game-sent messages.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "all the cards you collect disappear after 30 days. So you have to collect them all over again",
      "rationale": "Collected rewards expire on a game-set clock, forcing the player to complete the collection within the time window or lose progress."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "you have to collect them all over again",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly describes the compulsion and requirement to acquire the complete set of cards."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a gacha system in which real money buys tickets used for chance-based SSR pulls, with a stated 4% success chance and a 200-ticket guarantee. This supports M_IntermediateCurrency, M_Gambling, and P_RewardMania. M_PayToProgress is rejected because the review does not clearly state that obtaining the unit is required for game progress. P_IllusionOfControl is rejected because the failed pulls are reported as bad luck, not as hidden or manipulated outcome determination. P_CompleteTheCollection, M_WasteAversion, and other labels are not supported.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "You get about 20 tickets with $80",
      "rationale": "The span describes acquiring in-game tickets with real-world money."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "You get about 20 tickets with $80 so even being generous it's still $600-$800 just to guarantee the unit you want. 4% chance to pull any SSR at any time.",
      "rationale": "The span links real-money-funded tickets with a chance-based pull for a unit."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "Gacha is quite scummy.",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly identifies a gacha mechanic, involving unpredictable chance-based reward delivery."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
